	(text "8-key keypad that emits a real 8-N-1 serial frame per key event,\nbuilt only from 74HC glue logic and a diode ROM.  No MCU, no FPGA,\nno firmware, no programmable part of any kind.\n \nPRINCIPLE OF OPERATION\n  A 74HC161 sweeps a 3-bit address across the 8 keys at the bit clock,\n  so every key is sampled 1/8 of the baud rate (1200 Hz at 9600 baud).\n  74HC151 #1 reads the LIVE debounced key at that address; 74HC151 #2\n  reads the STORED state of the same address out of a 74HC259 latch.\n  A 74HC86 XORs them.  A difference sets the BUSY flip-flop on the\n  FALLING clock edge, which freezes the scan counter through its\n  synchronous count-enables (CEP/CET) - the clock itself is never\n  gated, so no runt pulses are possible.\n  While BUSY, the frozen address selects one row of a 74HC238 +\n  diode ROM, which presents 7 ASCII bits to a pair of 74HC165s.\n  The shift registers emit start / b0..b6 / make-break / stop over\n  the next 10 bit times.  A second 74HC161 + 74HC138 count the frame,\n  write the new key state into the 74HC259 at state 10, and release\n  BUSY one bit later.  Scanning resumes; N-key rollover falls out for\n  free because each pending change is serviced on a later sweep.\n \nOUTPUT       JP4 selects 3.3V TTL UART or RS-232 (MAX3232 -> RJ45).\nBAUD         JP2 taps the 74HC4060 divider; see sheet 2 for the table.\nASCII MAP    Set by which diodes are fitted; see sheet 6."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
